# BUG-084 regression fixture — a NEGATIVE test, and the parity it pins is the point.
#
# The selfhost Lexer tracked `[`/`]` in parenDepth; the Zig Tokenizer tracks only
# `(`/`)`. Since parenDepth is what suppresses end-of-line, the two front ends disagreed
# about whether a bracket could span lines: the selfhost accepted the form below and the
# bootstrap rejected it. The fix removed bracket tracking from the selfhost, so BOTH now
# suppress EOL only inside parentheses and multi-line `[...]` is consistently unsupported.
#
# WHY A MUST-FAIL FIXTURE IS THE RIGHT PIN. Reverting the fix makes the selfhost ACCEPT
# this file, and `smoke_tc_fail` is red the moment it does -- an acceptance is exactly
# what the divergence was. A positive fixture could not see it: everything that compiles
# today would still compile with the bug back.
#
# Verified against BOTH compilers when this was written (2026-08-20): selfhost says
# "unexpected expression token" at 23:17, the bootstrap "syntax error near". The messages
# differ; the ACCEPT/REJECT verdict is what has to agree, and does.
#
# If multi-line bracket literals are ever deliberately supported, this file is the thing
# that will go red -- and the right response then is to change BOTH lexers and delete it,
# not to loosen it.

def main()
    var xs = [1,
        2]
    print(xs.len)
